<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Claus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kris Kringle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Claus is a legendary figure originating in Western Christian culture who is said to bring gifts on the night of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Eve. The figure derives from folklore surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, the 4th-century Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> and patron saint of children, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinterklaas">Sinterklaas</a>, whose legend settlers brought to 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>; it also merged with the English Father Christmas. The name was first used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> press in 1773. The 1823 poem &quot;A Visit from St. Nicholas&quot;, with its sleigh pulled by flying reindeer, laid the foundation for the modern image, which the cartoonist Thomas Nast helped to define in Harper's Weekly from 1863.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-claus.jpg" alt="Santa Claus" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nast · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merry_Old_Santa_Claus_by_Thomas_Nast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The flags of New York City, Albany and Jersey City carry the colours of the Prinsenvlag, the feast of Saint Nicholas gave <b>Santa Claus</b>, and Dutch names such as Manhattan, Catskill and Red Hook remain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Santa Claus</b> derives from the folklore of Saint Nicholas, the fourth-century bishop of Myra, and from the Dutch Sinterklaas, whose legend settlers brought to New Amsterdam; the profile of New Netherland counts him among the colony's legacies.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers brought his legend to 17th-century New Amsterdam, and from it, from the folklore of Saint Nicholas and from the English Father Christmas came <b>Santa Claus</b>, who is said to bring gifts on the night of Christmas Eve.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
